    "turn_text": "You know, it's interesting. I think it's the first lesson of Judaism and obviously it becomes the first lesson of Christianity. If you think about trust, if we break it down into some of the key words and what they mean in the Hebrew, the word trust is to be confident, to be sure. That's one of the interesting things about translation. These are the verses that are translated into multiple languages over time. Sometimes can lose their meanings. You have to rely on someone that knows what the individual words meant to the people who wrote them and what word they chose. different words that could be translated the same way, but they lose their meaning in the translation. But the word trust, really, when it was written, it was written to be confident to be sure. The heart, we would think of as simply what we mean in contemporary society, the heart is your emotions, and that's certainly a part of it, and this is your feelings, but the word that they pick was a word that extends to beyond your feelings. It's your entire sort of decision-making basis. So it's your feelings, but it's also your will. It's also your intellect. So it's trusting these things, and then lean. And that's a word that probably the three, the one that loses the least of its meaning is really about support. And a lot of this has to do at the time, of course, in the era of the Kings that they would rely or lean on a series of advisors of different people who surrounded the King and helped the King make decisions and so forth. And so what this is basically saying is that be confident and sure about your feelings, your will and your intellect in the Lord and lean on that for support for your decisions. Now, so that's the first lesson because if that doesn't happen, then nothing else makes sense. So if you go through the law or if you go through the 10 commandments as they were handed down, why would you believe any of them? if you don't trust in the source of those commandments. And in essence, those commandments in many ways, I view as the owner's manual, right? And you buy a car or where you buy one of those bikes that I see in your background here, how people can see it and how people can see it and how people can see",
