    "turn_text": "Yeah. So from the very beginning, you see the lesson in the story of Adam and Eve where they're told, you know, you can be fruitful, multiply this garden is yours, this beautiful earth, there's no death, there's no destruction, there's no pain. There's only one thing you can't do. I don't want you to eat from the fruit of the tree of knowledge. And of course, that's the first temptation, right? And that is, of course, he doesn't want you to have that, because then you'll be as smart as he is. Or why can't I be from the tree of knowledge? In essence, you begin your own understanding begins to tell you that the creator doesn't know what he's talking about. And that, of course, unleashes the events of human history. And then the other that I think is pretty dramatic is that in the Exodus, as their own understanding told them, there's no way we're going into that sea. I mean, we're all going to drown. And it's at that moment when the Lord has to act on their behalf. So despite all that had happened to that point, God had proven himself now repeatedly over a period of time. And these are people that have been in bondage for generations. They are let go, they are freed as a result of a series of miraculous events in which the Lord showed his hand. And then when they reach the sea's edge, their own understanding tells them, I'm not going in there because we're going to drown and we're going to die and we're trapped between Pharaoh's chariots on one side and the drowning and the sea. And it's where God proves himself again. Of course, as the Exodus continues, time and again, you see this and it's really symbolic of the story of humanity and that it's time and again, no matter how much the Lord has proven himself, we constantly find reason to doubt that he'll do it again. And so again, this passage speaks to that and it's throughout the stories that we find in the history of mankind.",
