  {
    "episode_id": 82325,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/2/82325.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 24,
    "turn_text": "I think first it's knowing God's teachings and really digging into it. I don't think there's any argument that can be made that the Bible is the most influential book in human history. All three major faiths rely on what Christians call the Old Testament. That's often forgotten, but in the Islamic tradition, the entire Old Testament is accepted as well as in Christianity. In fact, you can't really understand Christianity if you don't understand pre-Christianity. It's all built on that. We forget at the end of the day that that Jesus of Nazareth was a first century Jewish man living in Judea and Samaria. And so his worldview and all the words he said have meaning in the context of that experience and he was observant as well. Understanding teachings, the foundations of it are critical because you can't apply something you don't understand. I don't think you have to approach these things as superstition. I think you have to approach these things as practical. I think there is not a single instruction or a single commandment. that doesn't stand up to scrutiny as to being wise and the right thing to do. So I think that begins with that. And then they get to surround yourself with people that value that as well. And then I think you have to do the best you can using the gifts of intellect and wisdom that we've been given as rational creatures to apply them in our daily circumstances, understanding that we will fall short as many of the great prophets and figures in the Old Testament fell short. I mean, King David, was a phenomenal leader, certainly a historic one in the context of so much of what happened in the Old Testament, and yet he failed. But he was held accountable for his failures, and he was held accountable by someone who came to him and said, you know, what you've done is wrong with Bathsheba, and you need to atone for it, and there's going to be a consequence for it. So I think there's a couple of things. Understanding and teachings, understanding the meaning of them, and then having people around you that value those teachings and will hold you accountable to it are two really practical things that we can do in our daily lives. And then understand we'll fall short.",
