  {
    "episode_id": 82325,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/2/82325.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 30,
    "turn_text": "Yes, there's two things that happen. There's a weekly, what they call a prayer breakfast and it's really the, you know, cross denominational multi tradition. So we have the Jewish members are there and Christian members are there of various denominations. And it really is a time in which every week there's a speaker that's invited to come and sort of speaks about a passage, you know, the chaplain is involved but isn't really preaching anything of that nature. And then you sort of share your story or what have you. And then there's a conversation. So it's a good opportunity to kind of get to know your colleagues in ways that the public never does. I always tell people that, you know, the only thing you know about some of my colleagues is what you heard them say on CNN or in a debate or whatever it might be. I know them as a full person in many ways, in many cases. So I thought that's important. There is a weekly Bible study as well that I'm not a part of, frankly, because it comes in the middle of the day. But one of the things that I try to do every morning, and I don't always do it, but I try to every morning, and people notice on Twitter that I post a Bible verse and almost exclusively from the Old Testament because I wanted to speak to as many people as possible since something that all three faiths can rely on. Every now and then, of course, they'll have a new testament, for example, my choice today was from the Beatitudes. But generally, it's a very heavily on Proverbs, too. And so a lot of times, the media will see what I put up and think there's some sort of political context to it. There really isn't. It oftentimes just comes to what I read that morning.",
