  {
    "episode_id": 82325,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/2/82325.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 35,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think that's a great question. And I think those observations are important observations that I would share those, but I would add to it the following. I think, and this is hard to say, because some people, frankly, appear in our eyes to be beyond redemption, in many cases, but for the most part, except for the most disfigured souls at this point, people who, for a variety of different reasons, have gone into a direction that, from a human perspective, is irredeemable. There is something special about every person you'll ever meet. And what we value in society is special is different. I mean, you could argue that a neurosurgeon is more important for society and for the well-being of a country than a basketball player, but the basketball player makes substantially more money. And I don't mean the starting basketball player. I mean, like the 11th guy on the roster makes substantially more money. than any neurosurgeon in America. So everybody has something special about it, whether it's something that society values or not, whether it's something that will ever be talked about or not, something most special people, or people will never know about. I mean, they exist in anonymity and every single day they're making a difference in people's lives or in the world. So because there's something special about it, what we all have in common is that we have something special about us, whether society values are not. And which leads me to the second one. And that is everyone wants to be meaningful. In essence, everyone wants to matter. They want their time to matter. That's in Great Witch program in our DNA. It's part of our software. And that's why I believe you see people going to public service. That's why I see that after people make billions of dollars and become famous beyond their wildest dreams and have every material possession. They donate hundreds of millions of dollars in volunteer time and run for office because no matter how much money you make and how much material things you collect, you want to have an impact on people's lives. That's why I think you see people in sports and entertainment say, we don't just want to do sports or acting. It's not meaningful. What's meaningful is can I get involved in a college that's going to save so much lives. So I really think so much about happiness, which you talked about a moment ago, is the ability to find what our special, the talent or gift may be, and it may be, you know, you're really good at organizing potlucks and raising money for your, your, your, your synagogue or your church in parish or. and then applying it to something that's meaningful and makes a difference. When people are not able to do those two things, it's corrosive. And so I think so much about life and it's so much about what I tell my kids is ideally you want to find a job that allows you to do something that's meaningful and that speaks to your skills. But no matter what, I hope you'll have a job that allows you the time and the flexibility to employ your special gift or talent in something that's meaningful, whether that makes you famous or not, whether that makes, whether it's paid or not, whether it's recognized by society or not. And I think those are two things I've learned about everybody every once the matter they want to make a difference, and when they don't have a chance to make a difference when that's denied to them. oftentimes that expresses itself in very negative ways and very negative anti-social behavior. And I believe that explains a lot about what happens on a daily basis.",
